 { In any type of ending, interference with the enemy play is an important idea for the defender, and even more so in queen endings, considering the power of this piece. Here are some examples. }  
 *

[Event "Mastering Endgame Strategy"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/12630174/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "41.2"]
[White "5.6 Interference"]
[Black "Example 326- Alekhine- A. vs. Reshevsky- S.- AVRO Tournament- The Netherlands 1938"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[FEN "6k1/3q1p2/6pp/8/P7/5PP1/Q5KP/8 b - - 0 1"]

 { Black's task is difficult, but the fact that White has weakened his kingside with f2-f3 gives hope of a successful defence. } 1... { -KEY- } Qc6 $1 { Heading for the great square on a6, from which the queen will control the a-pawn and eye the opposite flank at the same time. } ( 1... Qa7 $5 { with the same idea is also possible, as noticed by c0rias. } ) 
2. a5 Qa6 3. g4 { Trying to create a shelter for the king on g3. } ( { a) After } 3. Qd5 Kg7 4. Qd4 Kg8 5. Qb6 Qe2 $1 6. Qf2 Qa6 { White hasn't made progress - Speelman. } ) 
 ( { b) If the queens were rooks, then a king march to the opposite flank would be White's natural plan, but here the king will have nowhere to hide from the checks; e.g. } 3. Kf2 Qa7 4. Ke2 Qa6 5. Ke3 Qa7 6. Kd3 Qa6 7. Kd4 Qa7 8. Kc4 Qa6 9. Kb4 Qd6 10. Ka4 Qc6 ) 
g5 { Denying White further expansion on the kingside. } 4. Kf2 Qd6 $1 { Some useful interference. } 5. Kf1 Qa6+ 6. Kg2 Kg7 { Marking time, awaiting White's next step. } 7. Qb2+ Kg8 8. Qb8+ Kg7 9. Qe5+ Kg8 10. Kf2 Qa7+ 11. Ke2 Qa6+ 12. Kd2 Qc4 $1 { Very clever, obstructing the way for White's king. } 13. Qf5 Qd4+ 14. Ke2 Qb2+ 15. Kd3 Qb3+ 16. Ke2 Qb2+ 
 1/2-1/2

