[FEN "8/8/5pk1/3q3p/PQ4pP/6P1/5P2/6K1 b - - 0 1"]

 { Here White's king is much better protected than in the previous example, so Black has to look for alternative ways of interfering with the opponent's play. } 1... { -KEY- } Qd1+ 2. Kh2 Qc2 3. Qd4 Qc7 { We will soon see that, from here, the queen has better chances of creating counterplay than on a2, which, by the way, would be the right angle in a rook ending. } 4. Qe4+ Kg7 5. Qa8 $1 { White is ready to move the passed pawn forward, in similar fashion toExample 315 (Gelfand-Akopian). } f5 $1 { The only chance for counterplay lies in exposing the enemy king by ...f5-f4. } 6. Qd5 ( { The natural } 6. a5 { was stronger. Ribli might have omitted that after } f4 7. gxf4 Qxf4 8. Kg2 g3 { (else Black can't really get at White's king) there is the strong retreat } 9. Qf3 $1 { finishing off Black's counterplay } ) 
Qa7 $1 ( { Not } 6... f4 $2 7. Qd4 ( { or } 6... Qa7 6. Qg5 { With the text Spassky manages to take the a-pawn, thus limiting the battle to one single flank. } ) 
 ) 
7. Qxf5 Qxa4 8. Qxh5 Qe4 9. Qg5+ Kh7 10. h5 Qf3 $1 { Keeping up the active defence. } 11. Qg6+ Kh8 12. Qh6+ Kg8 13. Qf4 Qd1 14. Qg5+ ( { After } 14. h6 Kh7 15. Qg5 ( { or } 15. Qf6 ) 
Qd7 $1 { it won't be easy for White to win, since the pawn ending arising after the exchange on g7 is drawn unless he already has his king on f1, a scenario that Black will prevent by means of queen checks. } ) 
Kh7 15. Qf5+ Kg7 16. Qe4 { Ribli finally opts for a new plan: a timely f2-f4 to liberate his king. } Kh8 17. Kg2 Kg7 18. f4 $5 gxf3+ $1 ( { After } 18... Qd2 19. Kf1 Qd1 20. Kf2 Qd2 21. Qe2 Qd4 22. Qe3 Qb2 23. Ke1 Qb1 { it is not clear how White can make progress either. Nevertheless, the text is simpler, leaving the opponent with the 'bad' g- and h-pawns, a kind of theoretical draw that we already came across within Example 314 (Caruana-Dominguez). } ) 
19. Qxf3 Qc2+ 20. Kh3 Qd2 21. Qg4+ Kh8 22. h6 $6 { Tempting, but now Black can start looking for stalemate motifs. } ( { Instead, Ribli recommends } 22. Qe4 { preparing g3-g4, when } Qc1 23. g4 Qd2 24. Kh4 Kg7 25. g5 Qf2 26. Kg4 Qg1 27. Kf5 Qf2 28. Qf4 Qc2 { is one possible defence - Nalimov's tablebases. } ) 
Qb2 $1 ( { Not } 22... Qxh6 $2 23. Qh4 Qh7 24. Kg4 $1 { and White wins - Ribli. } ) 
23. Kh4 $6 ( { Something like } 23. Qe6 { would have been more promising in practice; e.g. } Qc3 24. Kh4 Kh7 { (for some reason, the only move) } 25. g4 { and here Black must find } Qc7 $1 { enabling a check on h2 - Nalimov's tablebases. } ) 
Qh2+ 24. Kg5 ( { Or } 24. Qh3 Qd2 $1 { - Ribli, when } 25. Kh5 { can be met by } Qd5 26. Kg6 Qe6 $1 ) 
Qxh6+ $1 
 1/2-1/2

