 { In the next example, a drawish outcome seems even more remote than in the previous one, but Black was finally rewarded for her stubborn defence. This example, alongside some of the previous ones in the present chapter, also highlights the fact that, in queen endings, a transposition to a pawn ending is always around the corner, due to the ease with which the queens can be exchanged. With two healthy extra pawns, it is time for White to look for the most technical path to victory. } 1. { -KEY- } Qd7+ $6 ( { Among all types of endings, the one where an extra pawn is most strongly felt is the pawn ending. Here } 1. Qe6 $1 Qxe6 2. dxe6 Kxe6 3. Kg3 { led to a trivial win, as long as White improves her king before advancing the pawns. There could follow } Kf5 4. Kf3 ( 4. Kh4 { also works - Baburin. } ) 
g5 5. Kg3 $1 ( { but not } 5. g3 $2 g4 $1 ) 
Kf6 6. Kg4 Kg6 7. g3 Kf6 8. Kh5 Kf5 9. g4 Kf6 10. Kh6 { and wins. } ) 
Kf6 2. Qd8+ Kf5 3. Qf8+ Kg5 4. Qf3 Qd4 $1 { Obviously, Black could not swap queens, so instead keeps her majesty in the centre. } 5. Qg3+ $6 ( { Again, the idea of sacrificing one of the pawns to reach a pawn ending was available: } 5. Qg4 $1 Qxg4 6. hxg4 Kf6 7. Kg3 g5 $1 8. d6 Ke6 9. Kf3 Kxd6 10. Ke4 Ke6 11. g3 $1 Kf6 12. Kd5 { and White soon wins, e.g. by a later waiting move with the g3-pawn - Baburin. } ) 
Kf6 6. Qd6+ Kg5 7. Qe7+ Kh6 8. Qf8+ Kh7 9. Qf7+ Kh6 10. Qf8+ Kh7 11. Qe7+ Kh6 12. d6 $1 { Correct, betting on the passed pawn without worrying about the enemy queen checks. } Qf4+ 13. Kg1 Qc1+ 14. Kf2 Qd2+ 15. Kg3 g5 ( { After } 15... Qd3 16. Kh2 { the king escapes, so Cmilyte prefers to create a mating threat. } ) 
16. Qf6+ Kh5 17. Qf7+ Kh6 18. Qe6+ Kh5 19. Qe5 $1 { Centralization. } Kh6 20. Kh2 $6 ( { An excellent alternative was } 20. h4 $1 { exposing the enemy king to future checks; e.g. } Qd3 ( { not } 20. Kh2 gxh4 21. Kxh4 Qxg2 $2 22. Qh8 $1 ) 
21. Kh2 gxh4 22. Qf6 { and the exchange of queens is imminent - Baburin. } ) 
Kh5 21. Qh8+ ( { A better choice was } 21. Qf6 { , for example } g4 22. hxg4 Kxg4 23. Qg6 Kh4 24. d7 $1 { and White wins, as shown by FSU70 / Stockfish. However, such variations are not easily visualized at the board when you are already beyond move ninety! } ) 
Kg6 22. Qe8+ Kh6 23. Qe6+ Kh5 24. Qg4+ Kh6 25. Qe6+ Kh5 26. Qe8+ $6 { This doesn't spoil anything yet, but it is a step in the wrong direction - Baburin. } ( { Correct was } 26. d7 $1 Qf4 27. Kg1 Qc1 28. Kf2 Qf4 ( { or } 28. Qe8+ Qd2 29. Qe2 { check } ) 
29. Ke2 { when the threat of Qe8+ and d8Q decides, as the white queen covers the crucial c4-square. } ) 
 ( { Another good option was } 26. Qf6 { transposing to the 21.Qf6 variation featured earlier in this example. } ) 
Kh6 27. d7 $2 { A fatal misevaluation; with the queen on e8, the king won't be able to escape the checks. } ( { Again } 27. Qe6 { followed by d6-d7, was called for. } ) 
Qf4+ 28. Kg1 Qc1+ 29. Kf2 Qd2+ { The rest is rather obvious, with the white king being short of shelter. } 30. Kf3 Qd3+ 31. Kg4 Qd4+ 32. Kf5 Qf4+ 33. Ke6 Qe4+ 34. Kd6 Qd4+ 35. Ke6 Qe4+ 36. Kf6 Qf4+ 37. Ke6 Qe4+ 38. Kd6 Qd4+ 39. Kc6 Qc4+ 40. Kb6 Qb4+ 41. Ka6 Qa4+ 42. Kb7 Qb5+ 43. Kc7 Qc5+ 44. Kd8 Qa5+ $1 ( { But not } 44... Qb6 $2 45. Ke7 Qc5 46. Kf6 $1 Qf2 { (the queen should have been on f4 at this moment) } 47. Ke6 Qb6 48. Kf7 Qg6 49. Ke7 Qe4 50. Kf8 { and White wins - Baburin. } ) 
45. Kc8 Qc5+ 46. Kb7 Qb5+ 47. Kc8 Qc5+ 48. Kd8 Qa5+ 49. Ke7 Qe5+ 50. Kf7 Qf5+ 51. Ke7 ( { There was even a way to lose to the game: } 51. Kg8 $4 Qh7 52. Kf8 Qg7 ) 
Qe5+ 
 1/2-1/2

